1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,512
On the back of almost every Le

2
00:00:02,512 --> 00:00:06,113
Mans prototype from the last fifteen years,

3
00:00:06,113 --> 00:00:07,453
there is a tall,

4
00:00:07,453 --> 00:00:09,714
thin fin, like the fin of a

5
00:00:09,714 --> 00:00:10,216
shark.

6
00:00:10,396 --> 00:00:12,498
It does not make the car faster.

7
00:00:12,678 --> 00:00:15,258
It was added because racing cars were

8
00:00:15,258 --> 00:00:18,326
flying, literally, lifting off the ground at

9
00:00:18,326 --> 00:00:21,255
over three hundred kilometres per hour and

10
00:00:21,255 --> 00:00:23,347
somersaulting through the air.

11
00:00:23,527 --> 00:00:26,302
Today, ten parts of the cars that

12
00:00:26,302 --> 00:00:29,497
race for twenty-four hours at Le Mans,

13
00:00:29,497 --> 00:00:31,515
and why each one exists.

14
00:00:31,864 --> 00:00:34,597
The twenty-four hours of Le Mans is

15
00:00:34,597 --> 00:00:37,642
the oldest endurance race in the world,

16
00:00:37,642 --> 00:00:40,375
first run in nineteen twenty-three.

17
00:00:40,555 --> 00:00:43,664
Three drivers share one car,

18
00:00:43,664 --> 00:00:45,219
day and night,

19
00:00:45,219 --> 00:00:46,551
rain or dry,

20
00:00:46,551 --> 00:00:48,217
for a full day.

21
00:00:48,397 --> 00:00:50,535
The cars must be fast like a

22
00:00:50,535 --> 00:00:52,978
Formula One car and tough like a

23
00:00:52,978 --> 00:00:53,436
truck.

24
00:00:53,616 --> 00:00:56,044
Many ideas that are now in your

25
00:00:56,044 --> 00:00:58,630
road car were tested there first,

26
00:00:58,630 --> 00:01:01,371
from disc brakes to hybrid systems.

27
00:01:01,721 --> 00:01:04,893
As always, every fact here comes from

28
00:01:04,893 --> 00:01:06,179
a named source,

29
00:01:06,179 --> 00:01:08,408
the organisers of Le Mans,

30
00:01:08,408 --> 00:01:13,465
the World Endurance Championship and motorsport references,

31
00:01:13,465 --> 00:01:16,037
all listed in the description.

32
00:01:16,217 --> 00:01:17,621
Let's start with the fin.

33
00:01:17,972 --> 00:01:19,927
Why do Le Mans cars have a

34
00:01:19,927 --> 00:01:21,280
fin on their back?

35
00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:23,655
To stop them from flying.

36
00:01:23,835 --> 00:01:26,930
A modern prototype has a very flat,

37
00:01:26,930 --> 00:01:29,759
wide body that works like a wing

38
00:01:29,759 --> 00:01:31,439
turned upside down.

39
00:01:31,619 --> 00:01:33,481
When the car goes straight,

40
00:01:33,481 --> 00:01:35,067
the air pushes it down.

41
00:01:35,247 --> 00:01:37,674
But if the car spins and slides

42
00:01:37,674 --> 00:01:40,491
sideways or backwards at high speed,

43
00:01:40,491 --> 00:01:42,918
the same body can start to work

44
00:01:42,918 --> 00:01:44,248
like a real wing,

45
00:01:44,248 --> 00:01:46,753
and lift the car off the ground.

46
00:01:47,103 --> 00:01:50,291
The most famous case happened in nineteen

47
00:01:50,291 --> 00:01:51,224
ninety-nine.

48
00:01:51,404 --> 00:01:54,460
The Mercedes CLR lifted off three times

49
00:01:54,460 --> 00:01:55,479
that weekend.

50
00:01:55,659 --> 00:01:58,401
Mark Webber's car flipped over twice,

51
00:01:58,401 --> 00:02:00,698
in practice and in the warm up.

52
00:02:00,878 --> 00:02:02,112
Then in the race,

53
00:02:02,112 --> 00:02:04,581
Peter Dumbreck's car took off on a

54
00:02:04,581 --> 00:02:07,921
straight, somersaulted and landed in the trees

55
00:02:07,921 --> 00:02:09,156
beside the track.

56
00:02:09,336 --> 00:02:12,505
Amazingly, every driver survived.

57
00:02:12,685 --> 00:02:16,284
Mercedes withdrew its last car and left

58
00:02:16,284 --> 00:02:17,945
sports car racing.

59
00:02:18,295 --> 00:02:20,424
The fin works like the feathers on

60
00:02:20,424 --> 00:02:20,988
an arrow.

61
00:02:21,168 --> 00:02:24,292
When the car starts to turn sideways,

62
00:02:24,292 --> 00:02:26,909
the air hits the fin and pushes

63
00:02:26,909 --> 00:02:28,936
the tail back into line,

64
00:02:28,936 --> 00:02:32,059
so the car keeps pointing forward and

65
00:02:32,059 --> 00:02:34,508
the body keeps pressing down.

66
00:02:34,688 --> 00:02:37,006
After more flying accidents,

67
00:02:37,006 --> 00:02:40,650
the rules made fins compulsory on prototypes

68
00:02:40,650 --> 00:02:42,223
from twenty eleven.

69
00:02:42,403 --> 00:02:44,465
It is a safety part that looks

70
00:02:44,465 --> 00:02:45,839
like a style choice.

71
00:02:46,190 --> 00:02:48,643
Why do Le Mans cars have holes

72
00:02:48,643 --> 00:02:51,016
and slats on top of the wheel

73
00:02:51,016 --> 00:02:51,588
arches?

74
00:02:51,768 --> 00:02:54,883
Because the spinning wheels pump air into

75
00:02:54,883 --> 00:02:56,099
the wheel wells.

76
00:02:56,279 --> 00:02:58,865
A tyre turning at high speed drags

77
00:02:58,865 --> 00:03:00,310
air around with it,

78
00:03:00,310 --> 00:03:03,048
and that air gets trapped inside the

79
00:03:03,048 --> 00:03:03,732
bodywork.

80
00:03:03,912 --> 00:03:06,473
Trapped air means high pressure,

81
00:03:06,473 --> 00:03:09,594
and high pressure under the body pushes

82
00:03:09,594 --> 00:03:10,474
the car up,

83
00:03:10,474 --> 00:03:12,875
the opposite of what you want.

84
00:03:13,226 --> 00:03:15,821
The louvres, those slats cut in the

85
00:03:15,821 --> 00:03:17,453
top of the wheel arch,

86
00:03:17,453 --> 00:03:19,530
let that air escape upwards.

87
00:03:19,710 --> 00:03:21,947
Less pressure inside,

88
00:03:21,947 --> 00:03:24,505
less lift and less drag.

89
00:03:24,685 --> 00:03:27,355
You will also see the same trick

90
00:03:27,355 --> 00:03:29,775
on some fast road cars and in

91
00:03:29,775 --> 00:03:31,360
many racing series.

92
00:03:31,540 --> 00:03:33,280
The holes look aggressive,

93
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:35,688
but they are simply a pressure valve

94
00:03:35,688 --> 00:03:37,427
shaped by the wind tunnel.

95
00:03:37,777 --> 00:03:39,541
They also help with safety.

96
00:03:39,721 --> 00:03:41,597
If the car spins backwards,

97
00:03:41,597 --> 00:03:43,752
air can rush under the front of

98
00:03:43,752 --> 00:03:44,377
the body.

99
00:03:44,557 --> 00:03:47,079
The openings above the wheels let some

100
00:03:47,079 --> 00:03:49,202
of that air out before it builds

101
00:03:49,202 --> 00:03:51,326
enough pressure to lift the car.

102
00:03:51,506 --> 00:03:53,043
Together with the fin,

103
00:03:53,043 --> 00:03:55,140
the holes are part of the same

104
00:03:55,140 --> 00:03:58,564
lesson the sport learned in nineteen ninety-nine.

105
00:03:58,744 --> 00:04:01,182
A race car must never become a

106
00:04:01,182 --> 00:04:01,589
wing.

107
00:04:01,939 --> 00:04:04,403
Why did some old Le Mans cars

108
00:04:04,403 --> 00:04:06,188
have such long tails?

109
00:04:06,368 --> 00:04:08,318
Because of one straight.

110
00:04:08,498 --> 00:04:11,829
The Mulsanne, a public road closed for

111
00:04:11,829 --> 00:04:15,334
the race, was almost six kilometres long

112
00:04:15,334 --> 00:04:16,649
with no corner.

113
00:04:16,829 --> 00:04:20,333
Teams built special long tail versions of

114
00:04:20,333 --> 00:04:23,496
their cars, like the Porsche nine one

115
00:04:23,496 --> 00:04:25,974
seven LH, short for Langheck,

116
00:04:25,974 --> 00:04:27,684
long tail in German.

117
00:04:27,864 --> 00:04:30,576
A long, tapering tail lets the air

118
00:04:30,576 --> 00:04:32,970
close smoothly behind the car,

119
00:04:32,970 --> 00:04:34,087
and cuts drag.

120
00:04:34,437 --> 00:04:37,103
Less drag meant higher top speed on

121
00:04:37,103 --> 00:04:39,999
the straight, and the Porsche nine one

122
00:04:39,999 --> 00:04:42,818
seven long tails went well over three

123
00:04:42,818 --> 00:04:45,865
hundred fifty kilometres per hour in the

124
00:04:45,865 --> 00:04:47,770
early nineteen seventies,

125
00:04:47,770 --> 00:04:50,284
on tyres and brakes of that time.

126
00:04:50,464 --> 00:04:52,159
The price was stability.

127
00:04:52,339 --> 00:04:55,096
A long tail with little downforce could

128
00:04:55,096 --> 00:04:56,792
become nervous at speed,

129
00:04:56,792 --> 00:04:59,549
and drivers described the car as moving

130
00:04:59,549 --> 00:05:01,174
around on the straight.

131
00:05:01,525 --> 00:05:04,601
The idea came back again and again.

132
00:05:04,781 --> 00:05:07,169
In the late nineties and in the

133
00:05:07,169 --> 00:05:09,942
twenty tens, Le Mans cars still used

134
00:05:09,942 --> 00:05:12,870
special low drag bodywork for this one

135
00:05:12,870 --> 00:05:15,643
race, different from the rest of the

136
00:05:15,643 --> 00:05:16,182
season.

137
00:05:16,362 --> 00:05:19,106
Le Mans is so different from other

138
00:05:19,106 --> 00:05:22,253
tracks that teams design a separate car

139
00:05:22,253 --> 00:05:22,817
for it.

140
00:05:22,997 --> 00:05:25,536
The tail tells you which track the

141
00:05:25,536 --> 00:05:26,805
car is built for.

142
00:05:27,156 --> 00:05:29,267
Why are there two tight chicanes in

143
00:05:29,267 --> 00:05:31,440
the middle of the Mulsanne straight?

144
00:05:31,620 --> 00:05:34,441
Because the cars became too fast.

145
00:05:34,621 --> 00:05:36,747
In nineteen eighty-eight,

146
00:05:36,747 --> 00:05:39,979
a WM Peugeot prototype driven by Roger

147
00:05:39,979 --> 00:05:43,297
Dorchy was clocked at four hundred five

148
00:05:43,297 --> 00:05:46,359
kilometres per hour on the straight.

149
00:05:46,539 --> 00:05:49,225
That is still the fastest speed ever

150
00:05:49,225 --> 00:05:50,718
recorded at Le Mans.

151
00:05:51,069 --> 00:05:52,261
At those speeds,

152
00:05:52,261 --> 00:05:54,719
a tyre failure or a small mistake

153
00:05:54,719 --> 00:05:55,836
could be fatal,

154
00:05:55,836 --> 00:05:58,146
and the brakes and tyres of the

155
00:05:58,146 --> 00:06:00,008
time were at their limit.

156
00:06:00,188 --> 00:06:01,527
In nineteen ninety,

157
00:06:01,527 --> 00:06:04,063
two chicanes were added to break the

158
00:06:04,063 --> 00:06:06,458
straight into three shorter parts.

159
00:06:06,638 --> 00:06:09,230
The governing body would no longer approve

160
00:06:09,230 --> 00:06:11,328
circuits with straights that long.

161
00:06:11,508 --> 00:06:14,558
Top speeds dropped by dozens of kilometres

162
00:06:14,558 --> 00:06:15,212
per hour.

163
00:06:15,562 --> 00:06:18,393
The chicanes also changed how the cars

164
00:06:18,393 --> 00:06:19,138
are built.

165
00:06:19,318 --> 00:06:21,297
With more braking zones,

166
00:06:21,297 --> 00:06:25,092
brakes and stability under braking became more

167
00:06:25,092 --> 00:06:28,475
important, and the special top speed cars

168
00:06:28,475 --> 00:06:29,465
disappeared.

169
00:06:29,645 --> 00:06:32,046
The famous straight still exists,

170
00:06:32,046 --> 00:06:33,865
and you can drive it on a

171
00:06:33,865 --> 00:06:36,340
normal day, because it is a public

172
00:06:36,340 --> 00:06:36,703
road.

173
00:06:36,883 --> 00:06:39,451
But the record of nineteen eighty-eight will

174
00:06:39,451 --> 00:06:41,260
probably never be broken there.

175
00:06:41,611 --> 00:06:44,400
Why do Le Mans cars lift themselves

176
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:46,870
off the ground in the pit stop?

177
00:06:47,050 --> 00:06:49,233
They have air jacks built in.

178
00:06:49,413 --> 00:06:52,911
Small pneumatic legs are hidden inside the

179
00:06:52,911 --> 00:06:53,244
car.

180
00:06:53,424 --> 00:06:56,019
A mechanic plugs a high pressure air

181
00:06:56,019 --> 00:06:58,542
lance into a valve on the bodywork,

182
00:06:58,542 --> 00:07:00,849
and the legs shoot down and lift

183
00:07:00,849 --> 00:07:02,723
the whole car in a second.

184
00:07:02,903 --> 00:07:04,958
No separate jack is needed.

185
00:07:05,309 --> 00:07:06,899
In endurance racing,

186
00:07:06,899 --> 00:07:09,760
the rules allow only a few mechanics

187
00:07:09,760 --> 00:07:11,748
to work on the car at the

188
00:07:11,748 --> 00:07:14,451
same time, and the car needs fuel,

189
00:07:14,451 --> 00:07:16,994
tyres and a driver change in the

190
00:07:16,994 --> 00:07:17,789
same stop.

191
00:07:17,969 --> 00:07:20,889
Carrying heavy jacks around the car would

192
00:07:20,889 --> 00:07:21,673
waste time.

193
00:07:21,853 --> 00:07:22,967
With air jacks,

194
00:07:22,967 --> 00:07:25,419
one person lifts the car with one

195
00:07:25,419 --> 00:07:27,944
movement, and the car drops to the

196
00:07:27,944 --> 00:07:30,693
ground the moment the lance is pulled

197
00:07:30,693 --> 00:07:30,990
out.

198
00:07:31,340 --> 00:07:33,991
Air jacks add weight and complexity,

199
00:07:33,991 --> 00:07:36,494
and Formula One does not use them.

200
00:07:36,674 --> 00:07:38,479
But in endurance racing,

201
00:07:38,479 --> 00:07:41,036
where cars stop dozens of times in

202
00:07:41,036 --> 00:07:43,141
one race, they are worth it.

203
00:07:43,321 --> 00:07:45,366
Over twenty-four hours,

204
00:07:45,366 --> 00:07:48,300
a second saved in every stop adds

205
00:07:48,300 --> 00:07:50,078
up to a lap or more.

206
00:07:50,258 --> 00:07:52,516
Le Mans can be decided by less

207
00:07:52,516 --> 00:07:53,570
than a minute,

208
00:07:53,570 --> 00:07:56,655
after more than five thousand kilometres.

209
00:07:57,006 --> 00:07:59,420
Why do Le Mans cars have glowing

210
00:07:59,420 --> 00:08:01,081
panels on their sides?

211
00:08:01,261 --> 00:08:03,199
At night, the track is dark,

212
00:08:03,199 --> 00:08:05,761
and more than sixty cars of different

213
00:08:05,761 --> 00:08:06,938
classes share it.

214
00:08:07,118 --> 00:08:10,051
The lit number panels let the marshals,

215
00:08:10,051 --> 00:08:12,833
the timing team and the fans identify

216
00:08:12,833 --> 00:08:13,585
every car.

217
00:08:13,765 --> 00:08:15,855
The colours show the class.

218
00:08:16,035 --> 00:08:19,288
In the World Endurance Championship,

219
00:08:19,288 --> 00:08:21,908
red is for the top Hypercars,

220
00:08:21,908 --> 00:08:23,173
blue for LMP2,

221
00:08:23,173 --> 00:08:25,613
and orange for the GT cars.

222
00:08:25,963 --> 00:08:29,229
Many cars also carry small LED displays

223
00:08:29,229 --> 00:08:32,496
that show their current position in the

224
00:08:32,496 --> 00:08:32,998
class.

225
00:08:33,178 --> 00:08:35,258
The leader carries a one,

226
00:08:35,258 --> 00:08:37,005
the second car a two,

227
00:08:37,005 --> 00:08:37,836
and so on,

228
00:08:37,836 --> 00:08:40,249
updated live during the race.

229
00:08:40,429 --> 00:08:42,677
For a driver coming up behind,

230
00:08:42,677 --> 00:08:44,550
it is useful information.

231
00:08:44,730 --> 00:08:47,108
Is the car in front fighting for

232
00:08:47,108 --> 00:08:48,966
a podium, or is it a back

233
00:08:48,966 --> 00:08:51,046
marker that will move aside?

234
00:08:51,396 --> 00:08:53,876
Mixed classes are the heart of Le

235
00:08:53,876 --> 00:08:54,252
Mans.

236
00:08:54,432 --> 00:08:57,177
The fastest cars lap much quicker than

237
00:08:57,177 --> 00:09:00,283
the slowest, so they are always overtaking.

238
00:09:00,463 --> 00:09:03,378
A Hypercar driver might pass a hundred

239
00:09:03,378 --> 00:09:05,142
cars in a single stint.

240
00:09:05,322 --> 00:09:08,470
Lights, colours and numbers turn a dark

241
00:09:08,470 --> 00:09:12,021
track full of strangers into something every

242
00:09:12,021 --> 00:09:14,604
driver can read in a fraction of

243
00:09:14,604 --> 00:09:15,330
a second.

244
00:09:15,680 --> 00:09:18,645
Why do three drivers share one Le

245
00:09:18,645 --> 00:09:19,453
Mans car?

246
00:09:19,633 --> 00:09:22,045
Because no human can drive at the

247
00:09:22,045 --> 00:09:24,091
limit for twenty-four hours.

248
00:09:24,271 --> 00:09:26,670
The rules set a maximum time for

249
00:09:26,670 --> 00:09:29,368
each driver, and a minimum time too.

250
00:09:29,548 --> 00:09:31,575
So during the pit stop,

251
00:09:31,575 --> 00:09:34,482
one driver jumps out and the next

252
00:09:34,482 --> 00:09:35,627
one jumps in,

253
00:09:35,627 --> 00:09:38,094
often in less than a minute,

254
00:09:38,094 --> 00:09:40,473
while the car is refuelled.

255
00:09:40,823 --> 00:09:43,332
The drivers are often different heights,

256
00:09:43,332 --> 00:09:45,026
but there is only one seat.

257
00:09:45,206 --> 00:09:47,763
So each driver has a custom foam

258
00:09:47,763 --> 00:09:50,001
insert, moulded to his body,

259
00:09:50,001 --> 00:09:52,079
that slides into the seat.

260
00:09:52,259 --> 00:09:54,733
The pedals are often adjustable,

261
00:09:54,733 --> 00:09:56,898
and the belts are set so the

262
00:09:56,898 --> 00:09:59,760
outgoing driver can help strap in the

263
00:09:59,760 --> 00:10:00,456
next one.

264
00:10:00,636 --> 00:10:04,752
Teams practice driver changes as carefully as

265
00:10:04,752 --> 00:10:05,941
tyre changes.

266
00:10:06,291 --> 00:10:08,056
It used to be even harder.

267
00:10:08,236 --> 00:10:09,835
In the early decades,

268
00:10:09,835 --> 00:10:13,035
some drivers raced most of the twenty-four

269
00:10:13,035 --> 00:10:16,463
hours alone, and in nineteen fifty-two Pierre

270
00:10:16,463 --> 00:10:19,205
Levegh tried to drive the whole race

271
00:10:19,205 --> 00:10:20,043
by himself.

272
00:10:20,223 --> 00:10:22,672
He led until about an hour before

273
00:10:22,672 --> 00:10:25,120
the end, when exhaustion led to a

274
00:10:25,120 --> 00:10:27,050
mistake and the car broke.

275
00:10:27,230 --> 00:10:30,539
Today, rest is part of the strategy.

276
00:10:30,888 --> 00:10:33,651
Why can the electric motor of some

277
00:10:33,651 --> 00:10:36,333
Le Mans hybrids only push the car

278
00:10:36,333 --> 00:10:38,121
above a certain speed?

279
00:10:38,301 --> 00:10:40,724
Cars like the Toyota GR zero one

280
00:10:40,724 --> 00:10:43,527
zero hybrid have an electric motor on

281
00:10:43,527 --> 00:10:44,814
the front wheels.

282
00:10:44,994 --> 00:10:47,352
That gives four wheel drive when the

283
00:10:47,352 --> 00:10:48,466
motor is working.

284
00:10:48,646 --> 00:10:51,056
And four wheel drive is a huge

285
00:10:51,056 --> 00:10:54,913
advantage when accelerating out of slow corners,

286
00:10:54,913 --> 00:10:56,761
especially in the rain.

287
00:10:57,111 --> 00:10:59,799
To keep the racing fair against cars

288
00:10:59,799 --> 00:11:02,189
that only drive the rear wheels,

289
00:11:02,189 --> 00:11:04,654
the rules say the front motor can

290
00:11:04,654 --> 00:11:06,745
only work above a set speed,

291
00:11:06,745 --> 00:11:10,105
around one hundred ninety kilometres per hour

292
00:11:10,105 --> 00:11:10,927
in the dry.

293
00:11:11,107 --> 00:11:12,571
Below that speed,

294
00:11:12,571 --> 00:11:15,499
the hybrid car is rear wheel drive

295
00:11:15,499 --> 00:11:16,877
like the others.

296
00:11:17,057 --> 00:11:20,221
Above it, the electric motor adds its

297
00:11:20,221 --> 00:11:22,189
power on the straights.

298
00:11:22,539 --> 00:11:24,979
It is one of the strangest rules

299
00:11:24,979 --> 00:11:26,199
in motor racing,

300
00:11:26,199 --> 00:11:28,715
and it shows how the class works.

301
00:11:28,895 --> 00:11:30,426
Very different cars,

302
00:11:30,426 --> 00:11:34,558
built by different manufacturers with different ideas,

303
00:11:34,558 --> 00:11:36,012
must be made equal.

304
00:11:36,192 --> 00:11:39,694
Engineers design the most efficient car they

305
00:11:39,694 --> 00:11:40,012
can.

306
00:11:40,192 --> 00:11:42,601
Then the rule makers decide how and

307
00:11:42,601 --> 00:11:44,940
when each part is allowed to help.

308
00:11:45,291 --> 00:11:47,898
Why do Le Mans cars carry extra

309
00:11:47,898 --> 00:11:49,412
weight on purpose?

310
00:11:49,592 --> 00:11:52,379
It is called balance of performance.

311
00:11:52,559 --> 00:11:55,123
Because the cars in the top class

312
00:11:55,123 --> 00:11:56,443
are so different,

313
00:11:56,443 --> 00:11:58,230
with different engines,

314
00:11:58,230 --> 00:12:00,250
hybrid systems and shapes,

315
00:12:00,250 --> 00:12:03,358
the organisers adjust them to bring them

316
00:12:03,358 --> 00:12:03,902
closer.

317
00:12:04,082 --> 00:12:06,497
A car that is too fast may

318
00:12:06,497 --> 00:12:08,169
get extra ballast,

319
00:12:08,169 --> 00:12:11,605
less power, or less energy per stint.

320
00:12:11,955 --> 00:12:14,789
The ballast is usually heavy metal plates

321
00:12:14,789 --> 00:12:16,309
bolted low in the car,

322
00:12:16,309 --> 00:12:18,452
where they do the least harm to

323
00:12:18,452 --> 00:12:19,350
the handling.

324
00:12:19,530 --> 00:12:22,773
The numbers are published before each race

325
00:12:22,773 --> 00:12:25,012
and change during the season,

326
00:12:25,012 --> 00:12:27,251
based on data from every car.

327
00:12:27,431 --> 00:12:30,042
Teams spend a lot of time arguing

328
00:12:30,042 --> 00:12:32,969
about it, because a few kilograms can

329
00:12:32,969 --> 00:12:35,500
mean tenths of a second per lap.

330
00:12:35,850 --> 00:12:38,938
Critics say it punishes good engineering.

331
00:12:39,118 --> 00:12:41,334
Supporters say it is the only way

332
00:12:41,334 --> 00:12:44,221
to have many manufacturers fighting for the

333
00:12:44,221 --> 00:12:45,631
win at the same time.

334
00:12:45,811 --> 00:12:48,540
Either way, the result is visible.

335
00:12:48,720 --> 00:12:51,770
Le Mans now has more top class

336
00:12:51,770 --> 00:12:55,939
manufacturers than it has had in decades,

337
00:12:55,939 --> 00:12:58,481
Toyota, Ferrari, Porsche,

338
00:12:58,481 --> 00:13:01,735
Cadillac, Peugeot, BMW and more.

339
00:13:02,084 --> 00:13:04,254
Why did Le Mans drivers once run

340
00:13:04,254 --> 00:13:06,491
across the track to their cars at

341
00:13:06,491 --> 00:13:07,169
the start?

342
00:13:07,349 --> 00:13:09,764
This was the famous Le Mans start.

343
00:13:09,944 --> 00:13:11,815
The cars were lined up at an

344
00:13:11,815 --> 00:13:13,886
angle on one side of the track,

345
00:13:13,886 --> 00:13:16,158
and the drivers stood on the other

346
00:13:16,158 --> 00:13:16,492
side.

347
00:13:16,672 --> 00:13:18,604
When the flag dropped,

348
00:13:18,604 --> 00:13:20,361
they ran, jumped in,

349
00:13:20,361 --> 00:13:23,348
started the engine and drove away.

350
00:13:23,528 --> 00:13:25,319
It was spectacular,

351
00:13:25,319 --> 00:13:26,639
and dangerous.

352
00:13:26,989 --> 00:13:27,998
To save time,

353
00:13:27,998 --> 00:13:30,949
many drivers did not fasten their seat

354
00:13:30,949 --> 00:13:33,978
belts properly, planning to do it later

355
00:13:33,978 --> 00:13:35,220
on the straight.

356
00:13:35,400 --> 00:13:37,318
In nineteen sixty-nine,

357
00:13:37,318 --> 00:13:40,736
Jacky Ickx protested by walking slowly to

358
00:13:40,736 --> 00:13:44,070
his Ford, fastening his belts carefully,

359
00:13:44,070 --> 00:13:45,571
and starting last.

360
00:13:45,751 --> 00:13:47,176
On the first lap,

361
00:13:47,176 --> 00:13:50,697
driver John Woolfe crashed and was killed.

362
00:13:50,877 --> 00:13:52,792
Ickx went on to win the race.

363
00:13:53,142 --> 00:13:55,777
The running start was dropped after that.

364
00:13:55,957 --> 00:13:57,518
In nineteen seventy,

365
00:13:57,518 --> 00:14:01,185
drivers started already strapped in their cars,

366
00:14:01,185 --> 00:14:04,462
and from nineteen seventy-one Le Mans used

367
00:14:04,462 --> 00:14:07,115
a rolling start behind a pace car.

368
00:14:07,295 --> 00:14:09,991
Today the only running at the start

369
00:14:09,991 --> 00:14:11,532
is done by the fans.

370
00:14:11,712 --> 00:14:14,209
One walk across a track was enough

371
00:14:14,209 --> 00:14:16,926
to change a tradition of almost fifty

372
00:14:16,926 --> 00:14:17,366
years.

373
00:14:17,716 --> 00:14:19,225
So here are the ten.

374
00:14:19,405 --> 00:14:22,111
A shark fin that stops cars from

375
00:14:22,111 --> 00:14:22,702
flying.

376
00:14:22,882 --> 00:14:25,982
Holes above the wheels that release pressure.

377
00:14:26,162 --> 00:14:28,670
Long tails for a long straight,

378
00:14:28,670 --> 00:14:31,015
and chicanes to slow it down.

379
00:14:31,195 --> 00:14:33,141
Air jacks for fast stops,

380
00:14:33,141 --> 00:14:35,399
glowing panels for the night,

381
00:14:35,399 --> 00:14:37,500
one seat for three drivers,

382
00:14:37,500 --> 00:14:40,224
a hybrid that waits for high speed,

383
00:14:40,224 --> 00:14:42,404
hidden ballast for fairness,

384
00:14:42,404 --> 00:14:45,128
and a running start that ended with

385
00:14:45,128 --> 00:14:45,673
a walk.

386
00:14:46,023 --> 00:14:48,299
Le Mans is not only a race.

387
00:14:48,479 --> 00:14:50,902
It is a laboratory that runs for

388
00:14:50,902 --> 00:14:54,084
twenty-four hours, and every part of these

389
00:14:54,084 --> 00:14:56,583
cars has a story written by speed

390
00:14:56,583 --> 00:14:57,871
and by accidents.

391
00:14:58,051 --> 00:15:01,070
If you want more machines explained part

392
00:15:01,070 --> 00:15:03,937
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

393
00:15:04,117 --> 00:15:06,189
And tell us in the comments which

394
00:15:06,189 --> 00:15:08,262
machine you want us to take apart

395
00:15:08,262 --> 00:15:08,575
next.
